1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:18,580
Some of the advantages of bilingualism include better performance at tasks involving “executive function” (which involves the brain’s ability to plan and prioritize), better defense against dementia in old age and—the obvious—the ability to speak a second language.
双语能力的一些好处包括：在涉及“执行功能”（关乎大脑规划事务、分清主次的能力）的任务中表现更好；老年时更能抵御痴呆；还有显而易见的一点——会说第二语言。

2
00:00:19,380 --> 00:00:22,070
One purported advantage was not mentioned, though.
不过，有一个所谓的好处并没有提到。

3
00:00:22,870 --> 00:00:30,140
Many multilinguals report different personalities, or even different worldviews, when they speak their different languages.
许多会说多种语言的人表示，说不同的语言时，自己会有不同的性格，甚至不同的世界观。

4
00:00:30,940 --> 00:00:37,920
It’s an exciting notion, the idea that one’s very self could be broadened by the mastery of two or more languages.
这是一个令人兴奋的观念：掌握两种或更多语言，可能会拓展一个人的自我本身。

5
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:44,880
In obvious ways (exposure to new friends, literature and so forth) the self really is broadened.
在一些显而易见的方面（接触新的朋友、文学等等），自我确实会得到拓展。

6
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:52,630
Yet it is different to claim—as many people do—to have a different personality when using a different language.
然而，像许多人那样，声称自己使用另一种语言时就有另一种性格，则是另一回事了。

7
00:00:53,430 --> 00:00:59,490
A former Economist colleague, for example, reported being ruder in Hebrew than in English.
例如，《经济学人》的一位前同事就说，自己说希伯来语时比说英语时更粗鲁。

8
00:01:00,290 --> 00:01:01,760
So what is going on here?
那么，这是怎么回事呢？

9
00:01:02,560 --> 00:01:12,530
Benjamin Lee Whorf, an American linguist who died in 1941, held that each language encodes a worldview that significantly influences its speakers.
1941年去世的美国语言学家本杰明·李·沃尔夫认为，每种语言都蕴含着一种世界观，深刻影响着说这种语言的人。

10
00:01:13,330 --> 00:01:21,650
Often called “Whorfianism”, this idea has its skeptics, including The Economist, which hosted a debate on the subject in 2010.
这一观点常被称作“沃尔夫主义”，也不乏怀疑者，其中就包括《经济学人》——该刊曾在2010年就这一话题举办过一场辩论。

11
00:01:22,450 --> 00:01:25,600
But there are still good reasons to believe language shapes thought.
但仍有充分的理由相信，语言会塑造思维。

12
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:32,030
This influence is not necessarily linked to the vocabulary or grammar of a second language.
这种影响未必与第二语言的词汇或语法有关。

13
00:01:32,830 --> 00:01:36,390
Significantly, most people are not symmetrically bilingual.
值得注意的是，大多数人的两种语言能力并不对称。

14
00:01:37,190 --> 00:01:43,340
Many have learned one language at home from parents, and another later in life, usually at school.
许多人在家里跟父母学会了一种语言，后来又学了另一种，通常是在学校里学的。

15
00:01:44,140 --> 00:01:51,970
So bilinguals usually have different strengths and weaknesses in their different languages—and they are not always best in their first language.
因此，双语者在不同的语言上通常各有长处和短处——而且他们并不总是在第一语言上表现最好。

16
00:01:52,770 --> 00:02:05,610
For example, when tested in a foreign language, people are less likely to fall into a cognitive trap (answering a test question with an obvious-seeming but wrong answer) than when tested in their native language.
例如，与用母语接受测试相比，人们用外语接受测试时，落入认知陷阱（用一个看似显而易见、实则错误的答案来回答试题）的可能性更小。

17
00:02:06,410 --> 00:02:10,600
In part this is because working in a second language slows down the thinking.
这在一定程度上是因为，用第二语言做事会让思考变慢。

18
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:14,080
No wonder people feel different when speaking them.
难怪人们说这些语言时会感觉不一样。

19
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:23,220
And no wonder they feel looser, more spontaneous, perhaps more assertive or funnier or blunter, in the language they were reared in from childhood.
也难怪他们说从小伴随自己长大的那种语言时，会觉得更放松、更随性，或许还更敢于表达自己、更风趣或更直率。

20
00:02:24,020 --> 00:02:27,340
What of “crib” bilinguals, raised in two languages?
那么，在两种语言环境中长大的“摇篮”双语者又如何呢？

21
00:02:28,140 --> 00:02:32,980
Even they do not usually have perfectly symmetrical competence in their two languages.
即便是他们，两种语言的能力通常也并不完全对称。

22
00:02:33,780 --> 00:02:42,950
But even for a speaker whose two languages are very nearly the same in ability, there is another big reason that person will feel different in the two languages.
但即使一个人的两种语言能力几乎完全相同，也还有另一个重要原因，会让他说这两种语言时感觉不一样。

23
00:02:43,750 --> 00:02:49,360
This is because there is an important distinction between bilingualism and biculturalism.
这是因为双语能力和双文化背景之间有一个重要区别。

24
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:52,450
Many bilinguals are not bicultural.
许多双语者并不兼具两种文化背景。

25
00:02:53,250 --> 00:02:54,100
But some are.
但也有一些人兼具两种文化背景。

26
00:02:54,900 --> 00:03:01,250
And of those bicultural bilinguals, we should be little surprised that they feel different in their two languages.
而这些兼具两种文化背景的双语者说这两种语言时感觉不一样，我们也就不必感到意外了。

27
00:03:02,050 --> 00:03:09,260
Experiments in psychology have shown the power of “priming”—small unnoticed factors that can affect behavior in big ways.
心理学实验已经证明了“启动效应”的威力——未被察觉的微小因素可能会对行为产生重大影响。

28
00:03:10,060 --> 00:03:15,490
Asking people to tell a happy story, for example, will put them in a better mood.
例如，请人们讲一个快乐的故事，就会让他们心情变好。

29
00:03:16,290 --> 00:03:19,130
The choice between two languages is a huge prime.
选择两种语言中的哪一种，本身就是一个强大的启动因素。

30
00:03:19,930 --> 00:03:28,000
Speaking Spanish rather than English, for a bilingual and bicultural Puerto Rican in New York, might conjure feelings of family and home.
对纽约一位兼通两种语言、兼具两种文化背景的波多黎各人来说，说西班牙语而不说英语，可能会唤起亲情和家的感觉。

31
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:32,800
Switching to English might prime the same person to think of school and work.
而改用英语，则可能会促使同一个人联想到学校和工作。

32
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:42,080
So there are two very good reasons (asymmetrical ability, and priming) that make people feel different speaking their different languages.
因此，人们说不同的语言时之所以会感觉不一样，有两个非常充分的原因（语言能力不对称和启动效应）。

33
00:03:42,880 --> 00:03:45,660
We are still left with a third kind of argument, though.
不过，我们还剩下第三类论点。

34
00:03:46,460 --> 00:03:55,980
An economist recently interviewed here at Prospero, Athanasia Chalari, said, for example: “Greeks are very loud and they interrupt each other very often.
例如，本博客Prospero最近采访过的一位经济学家阿塔纳西娅·哈拉里就说：“希腊人说话嗓门很大，而且动不动就互相打断。

35
00:03:56,780 --> 00:03:59,620
The reason for that is the Greek grammar and syntax.
原因就在于希腊语的语法和句法。

36
00:04:00,420 --> 00:04:11,500
When Greeks talk they begin their sentences with verbs and the form of the verb includes a lot of information so you already know what they are talking about after the first word and can interrupt more easily.”
希腊人说话时，句子都以动词开头，而动词的形式包含了大量信息，所以听到第一个词，你就已经知道他们在说什么了，也就更容易打断他们。”

37
00:04:12,300 --> 00:04:16,740
Is there something intrinsic to the Greek language that encourages Greeks to interrupt?
希腊语是否有某种内在的东西，促使希腊人去打断别人？

38
00:04:17,540 --> 00:04:19,240
Consider Johnson skeptical.
约翰逊（即本专栏作者）对此表示怀疑。

39
00:04:20,040 --> 00:04:26,780
People seem to enjoy telling tales about their languages’ inherent properties, and how they influence their speakers.
人们似乎很喜欢讲一些故事，说自己的语言有哪些固有特性，这些特性又如何影响说这种语言的人。

40
00:04:27,580 --> 00:04:39,200
A group of French intellectual worthies once proposed, rather self-flatteringly, that French be the sole legal language of the EU, because of its supposedly unmatchable rigor and precision.
一群法国知识界名流曾颇为自吹自擂地提议，让法语成为欧盟唯一的法律语言，理由是法语据说具有无与伦比的严谨性和精确性。

41
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:46,090
Some Germans believe that frequently putting the verb at the end of a sentence makes the language especially logical.
一些德国人认为，经常把动词放在句末，使得德语特别有逻辑性。

42
00:04:46,890 --> 00:05:04,230
But language myths are not always self-flattering: many speakers think their languages are unusually illogical or difficult—witness the plethora of books along the lines of “Only in English do you park on a driveway and drive on a parkway; English must be the craziest language in the world!”
但语言神话并不总是自我吹捧的：许多人觉得自己所说的语言格外不合逻辑或格外难——看看那些多得数不清、内容大致如下的书就知道了：“只有在英语里，你才会在driveway（私家车道）上park（停车），在parkway（林荫大道）上drive（开车）；英语一定是世界上最疯狂的语言！”

43
00:05:05,030 --> 00:05:10,540
What such pop-Whorfian stories share is a (natural) tendency to exoticize languages.
这类通俗版沃尔夫主义故事的共同之处，在于都（自然而然地）倾向于把语言异域化。

44
00:05:11,340 --> 00:05:20,840
We also see some unsurprising overlap with national stereotypes and self-stereotypes: French, rigorous; German, logical; English, playful.
我们还看到，这些说法与民族刻板印象和自我刻板印象有一些重合，这并不令人意外：法语严谨，德语有逻辑，英语俏皮。

45
00:05:21,640 --> 00:05:22,460
Of course.
当然如此。

46
00:05:23,260 --> 00:05:38,280
In this case, Ms. Chalari, a scholar, at least proposed a specific and plausible line of causation from grammar to personality: in Greek, the verb comes first, and it carries a lot of information, hence easy interrupting.
在这个例子中，学者哈拉里女士至少提出了一条从语法到性格的具体而说得通的因果链：在希腊语中，动词放在最前面，而且承载了大量信息，因此很容易打断别人。

47
00:05:39,080 --> 00:05:45,330
The problem is that many unrelated languages all around the world put the verb at the beginning of sentences.
问题在于，世界各地有许多彼此没有亲缘关系的语言，都把动词放在句首。

48
00:05:46,130 --> 00:05:52,250
Many languages all around the world are heavily inflected, encoding lots of information in verbs.
世界各地有许多语言屈折变化丰富，在动词里编码了大量信息。

49
00:05:53,050 --> 00:05:59,430
It would be a striking finding if all of these unrelated languages had speakers more prone to interrupting each other.
如果所有这些彼此没有亲缘关系的语言，其使用者都更容易互相打断，那将是一个惊人的发现。

50
00:06:00,230 --> 00:06:09,020
Welsh, for example, is also both verb-first and about as heavily inflected as Greek, but the Welsh are not known as pushy conversationalists.
例如，威尔士语也是动词居首，屈折变化的丰富程度也和希腊语差不多，但威尔士人并不以说话咄咄逼人著称。
